celeritas.options.ray_tracing.name=Ray Tracing
celeritas.options.ray_tracing.tooltip=Traces the world with your graphics card's ray tracing hardware to compute indirect light. Chunk geometry is built into acceleration structures and shaded with the same textures the normal renderer uses, so light bounces correctly off surfaces that are off screen or hidden behind others. Requires a Vulkan adapter with ray query support.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_quality.name=Ray Tracing Quality
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_quality.tooltip=Controls how many rays each pixel casts and how far they travel. Higher settings resolve small details and distant bounces more faithfully, but cost a great deal more per frame. Quality and above add a second bounce, which is what lets light turn a corner into an unlit room.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_scale.name=Ray Tracing Resolution
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_scale.tooltip=Resolution the trace runs at relative to the world. Indirect light changes slowly across a surface, so tracing at a fraction of the resolution and upscaling costs very little quality for a large saving. Full resolution is rarely worth it.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_indirect.name=Indirect Light
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_indirect.tooltip=How strongly light bouncing off other surfaces contributes. This is what carries colour from one block onto another and fills in what neither the sun nor the sky reaches directly.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_occlusion.name=Ambient Shaping
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_occlusion.tooltip=How directional the sky ambient term is. At zero the sky lights every surface equally; higher values favour upward-facing surfaces, which is closer to how a real sky dome behaves and gives blocks more shape.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_sky_light.name=Sky Light
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_sky_light.tooltip=How much light a ray brings back when it escapes to the sky. Lower this if interiors look washed out by daylight leaking through the ceiling; raise it for brighter, more open outdoor lighting.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_emissive.name=Emissive Strength
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_emissive.tooltip=Extra brightness given to blocks that emit light. Minecraft has no separate emission channel, so block light stands in for it. Raising this makes torches, lava and glowstone throw noticeably more light into the world.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_reflections.name=Ray Traced Reflections
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_reflections.tooltip=Casts a mirror ray from every surface. Because no material data exists, all surfaces are treated as plain dielectrics, so the effect is a grazing-angle sheen rather than a mirror finish. Costs roughly one extra ray per pixel.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_denoiser.name=Denoiser
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_denoiser.tooltip=Filters the noise a small ray budget produces. Temporal accumulates results across frames; Full adds an edge-stopping wavelet filter that spreads the remaining noise without crossing surfaces. Turning this off is only useful for seeing what the tracer actually produced.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_filter.name=Filter Iterations
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_filter.tooltip=Wavelet passes run by the denoiser, each reaching twice as far as the last. More passes give a smoother result at the cost of fine indirect detail.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_accumulation.name=Accumulation
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_accumulation.value=%s frames
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_accumulation.tooltip=Longest run of frames the denoiser will blend together. Higher values are cleaner and steadier when standing still, but lighting takes longer to catch up when it changes.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_radius.name=Traced Distance
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_radius.tooltip=Radius around you kept in the traceable scene. Geometry beyond it still renders normally but neither casts nor receives traced light. Lower values reduce both video memory use and the cost of rebuilding the scene.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_budget.name=Structure Build Budget
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_budget.tooltip=Chunk sections whose acceleration structures may be built in a single frame. Chunk changes arrive in bursts, so a lower budget spreads the work out and avoids stutter at the cost of traced light lagging behind edits.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_debug.name=Debug View
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_debug.tooltip=Replaces the image with one of the tracer's intermediate buffers. Useful for tuning the settings above and for seeing where noise or leaking is coming from.

