celeritas.options.ray_tracing_sun.name=Sunlight
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_sun.tooltip=Brightness of direct sunlight and moonlight. Unlike Minecraft's own lighting this is genuinely directional and genuinely shadowed, so raising it deepens shadows as much as it brightens what the sun reaches.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_sky_ambient.name=Sky Ambient
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_sky_ambient.tooltip=Brightness of the sky treated as a light source in its own right. This is what fills shadowed outdoor surfaces that the sun cannot reach. Lowering it makes shadows harsher and more contrasty.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_block_light.name=Block Light
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_block_light.tooltip=Brightness of Minecraft's block light, which still stands in for torches and lava. Once emissive blocks become real light sources this will be replaced by their actual contribution.
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_exposure.name=Exposure
celeritas.options.ray_tracing_exposure.tooltip=Overall exposure applied before tone mapping. Lighting is no longer clamped to Minecraft's light map, so this decides how the resulting range is mapped onto your display. Raise it if the world looks too dark, lower it if highlights are washing out.
